{"$ref":"#/texts/60"},{"$ref":"#/texts/61"},{"$ref":"#/texts/62"},{"$ref":"#/texts/63"},{"$ref":"#/texts/64"},{"$ref":"#/texts/65"},{"$ref":"#/texts/66"},{"$ref":"#/texts/67"},{"$ref":"#/texts/68"},{"$ref":"#/texts/69"},{"$ref":"#/texts/70"},{"$ref":"#/texts/71"},{"$ref":"#/texts/72"},{"$ref":"#/texts/73"},{"$ref":"#/texts/74"},{"$ref":"#/texts/75"},{"$ref":"#/texts/76"},{"$ref":"#/texts/77"},{"$ref":"#/texts/78"},{"$ref":"#/texts/79"},{"$ref":"#/texts/80"},{"$ref":"#/texts/81"},{"$ref":"#/texts/82"},{"$ref":"#/texts/83"},{"$ref":"#/texts/84"},{"$ref":"#/texts/85"},{"$ref":"#/texts/86"},{"$ref":"#/texts/87"},{"$ref":"#/texts/88"},{"$ref":"#/texts/89"},{"$ref":"#/texts/90"},{"$ref":"#/texts/91"},{"$ref":"#/texts/92"},{"$ref":"#/texts/93"},{"$ref":"#/texts/94"},{"$ref":"#/texts/95"},{"$ref":"#/texts/96"},{"$ref":"#/texts/97"},{"$ref":"#/texts/98"},{"$ref":"#/texts/99"},{"$ref":"#/texts/100"},{"$ref":"#/texts/101"},{"$ref":"#/texts/102"},{"$ref":"#/texts/103"},{"$ref":"#/texts/104"},{"$ref":"#/texts/105"},{"$ref":"#/texts/106"},{"$ref":"#/texts/107"},{"$ref":"#/texts/108"},{"$ref":"#/texts/109"},{"$ref":"#/texts/110"},{"$ref":"#/texts/111"},{"$ref":"#/texts/112"},{"$ref":"#/texts/113"},{"$ref":"#/texts/114"}],"content_layer":"body","footnotes":[],"label":"picture","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":141.25225830078125,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":75.40835571289062,"r":519.5093994140625,"t":231.2542724609375},"charspan":[0,0],"page_no":1}],"references":[],"self_ref":"#/pictures/0"}],"schema_name":"DoclingDocument","tables":[],"texts":[{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"7 Additional families of graphs","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":755.2382504366728,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.866,"r":326.084305,"t":769.5472858484375},"charspan":[0,31],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/0","text":"7 Additional families of graphs"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"7.1 Asteroidal triple-free graphs","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":726.0922043660845,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.866,"r":288.89954760000006,"t":738.0164282484375},"charspan":[0,33],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"7.1 Asteroidal triple-free graphs"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"An asteroidal triple in a graph G is a set T of 3 vertices such that G [ T ] is independent and every pair of vertices in T can be joined by a path that avoids the neighborhood of the third vertex in T . A graph G is asteroidal triple-free (AT-free) if G does not contain an asteroidal triple. Various classes of graphs are known to be AT-free, for example, interval graphs, cocomporability graphs and permutation graphs (see e.g. [COS97]). We show that all these classes have a bounded ratio by proving the following theorem.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":631.5665282484373,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999999,"r":524.4137903999997,"t":720.3176146484375},"charspan":[0,526],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"An asteroidal triple in a graph G is a set T of 3 vertices such that G [ T ] is independent and every pair of vertices in T can be joined by a path that avoids the neighborhood of the third vertex in T . A graph G is asteroidal triple-free (AT-free) if G does not contain an asteroidal triple. Various classes of graphs are known to be AT-free, for example, interval graphs, cocomporability graphs and permutation graphs (see e.g. [COS97]). We show that all these classes have a bounded ratio by proving the following theorem."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Theorem 7.1. For every asteroidal triple-free graph G , we have γ ( G ) ⩽ 3 · ρ ( G ) .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":607.5781969932649,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999999,"r":461.08618879999995,"t":618.3964162484373},"charspan":[0,87],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Theorem 7.1. For every asteroidal triple-free graph G , we have γ ( G ) ⩽ 3 · ρ ( G ) ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Proof. Our proof is based on the following property.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":582.7495282484373,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999999,"r":325.10046880000004,"t":599.2716146484373},"charspan":[0,52],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"Proof. Our proof is based on the following property."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Theorem 7.2. [COS97] Every connected asteroidal triple-free graph contains a pair of vertices such that any path between this pair of vertices is dominating.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":548.3001969932649,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999999,"r":524.4055599999996,"t":573.2894466484373},"charspan":[0,157],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Theorem 7.2. [COS97] Every connected asteroidal triple-free graph contains a pair of vertices such that any path between this pair of vertices is dominating."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Let G be an AT-free graph. By Theorem 7.2 there is a pair of vertices u, v ∈ V ( G ) such that any path between u and v is dominating. Let Π = π 1 , π 2 , . . . , π p be the shortest path between u and v . Thus, we have γ ( G ) ⩽ p . On the other hand as Π is a shortest path in G , by taking the set of vertices { π 3 i +1 | 0 ⩽ i ⩽ ⌊ p/ 3 ⌋ -1 } we get a packing in G . Therefore, ρ ( G ) ⩾ ⌊ p/ 3 ⌋ ⩾ ⌊ γ ( G ) / 3 ⌋ .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":469.67352824843715,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999987,"r":524.4094623999999,"t":543.9796146484373},"charspan":[0,421],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Let G be an AT-free graph. By Theorem 7.2 there is a pair of vertices u, v ∈ V ( G ) such that any path between u and v is dominating. Let Π = π 1 , π 2 , . . . , π p be the shortest path between u and v . Thus, we have γ ( G ) ⩽ p . On the other hand as Π is a shortest path in G , by taking the set of vertices { π 3 i +1 | 0 ⩽ i ⩽ ⌊ p/ 3 ⌋ -1 } we get a packing in G . Therefore, ρ ( G ) ⩾ ⌊ p/ 3 ⌋ ⩾ ⌊ γ ( G ) / 3 ⌋ ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"7.2 Convex graphs","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":435.23020436608454,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.866,"r":200.5699942,"t":447.1544282484375},"charspan":[0,17],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"7.2 Convex graphs"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"A bipartite graph G = ( X ∪ Y, E ) is convex if and only if one of the color classes, without loss of generality X , has an ordering such that for all y ∈ Y the vertices adjacent to y are consecutive in the ordering. We refer to this ordering as convex ordering .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":384.04152824843743,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999999,"r":524.4091936,"t":429.45561464843746},"charspan":[0,263],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"A bipartite graph G = ( X ∪ Y, E ) is convex if and only if one of the color classes, without loss of generality X , has an ordering such that for all y ∈ Y the vertices adjacent to y are consecutive in the ordering. We refer to this ordering as convex ordering ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Theorem 7.3. For every convex graph G , we have γ ( G ) ⩽ 3 · ρ ( G ) .","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":360.053196993265,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999999,"r":397.4371887999999,"t":370.8714162484374},"charspan":[0,71],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"Theorem 7.3. For every convex graph G , we have γ ( G ) ⩽ 3 · ρ ( G ) ."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Proof. We can assume that G does not have an isolated vertex. We will encode convex graphs using points and intervals in R . In particular, if the convex ordering of X is x 1 , . . . , x n , we use the embedding π : X → R defined as π ( x i ) = i , and for each y ∈ Y , we associate the interval ι ( y ) = { π ( x ) : x ∈ N G ( y ) } , see Figure 4 for an illustration. For subsets X ′ ⊆ X and Y ′ ⊆ Y , we use the notation π ( X ′ ) = { π ( x ) : x ∈ X ′ } and ι ( Y ′ ) = { ι ( y ) : y ∈ Y ′ } . In this encoding, the image of a set S ⊆ V ( G ) is a union of points and intervals, corresponding to the vertices in S ∩ X and S ∩ Y , respectively.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":248.54952824843735,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.86599999999981,"r":524.4131967999999,"t":351.7466146484374},"charspan":[0,647],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/10","text":"Proof. We can assume that G does not have an isolated vertex. We will encode convex graphs using points and intervals in R . In particular, if the convex ordering of X is x 1 , . . . , x n , we use the embedding π : X → R defined as π ( x i ) = i , and for each y ∈ Y , we associate the interval ι ( y ) = { π ( x ) : x ∈ N G ( y ) } , see Figure 4 for an illustration. For subsets X ′ ⊆ X and Y ′ ⊆ Y , we use the notation π ( X ′ ) = { π ( x ) : x ∈ X ′ } and ι ( Y ′ ) = { ι ( y ) : y ∈ Y ′ } . In this encoding, the image of a set S ⊆ V ( G ) is a union of points and intervals, corresponding to the vertices in S ∩ X and S ∩ Y , respectively."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"ι","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":223.07667841083742,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":406.7194921,"r":409.19157374,"t":234.88575905083746},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/11","text":"ι"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"(","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":221.12924719083742,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":409.19157374,"r":412.15273628,"t":238.68280531083747},"charspan":[0,1],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/12","text":"("},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","l